 new_table(d,'Table 4. Selected subgroup estimates at the 20% overall testing quota.',['Subgroup','n / events','FIB-4 referral','Boosting referral','FIB-4 capture','Boosting capture'],tab4,[1.35,.86,1.02,1.05,1.03,1.13], 'Unweighted n/events; all referral/capture percentages survey weighted. Stratification is descriptive, and event counts are insufficient to interpret every race/ethnicity category with precision.')
 figure(d,resdir,'fig5_equity_referral.png','Figure 5. Sex- and age-stratified referral opportunities under FIB-4 and gradient-boosting rankings. The 20% dashed quota applies to the whole eligible population, not necessarily within every subgroup.',5.75)
 diff_sex=r['equity_bootstrap_intervals']['Gradient boosting']['equal_sex_minus_global_capture']; equal=r['equal_opportunity_sex']['Gradient boosting']['case_capture'];
 p(d,f'Applying the 20% quota separately within males and females equalized referral rates by definition. Overall case capture under this illustrative sex-equalized boosting policy was {per(equal)}, compared with {per(bg)} for a single population-wide quota (difference {100*diff_sex["point_estimate"]:+.1f} percentage points; 95% CI {irng(diff_sex["ci95"])}). The interval encompasses modest increases and decreases; this policy is thus not evidence that equal referral rates improve case capture. Alternative ethically justified constraints may yield different trade-offs.')
 add_sub(d,'3.5. Sensitivity analysis and interpretable feature dependence')
 p(d,f'For the LSM ≥8 kPa endpoint ({r["further_descriptives"]["validation"]["outcome8_n"]} validation events), validation AUROC remained greater for both ML methods than FIB-4: {sens["FIB-4"]["auroc"]:.3f}, {sens["Logistic"]["auroc"]:.3f} and {sens["Gradient boosting"]["auroc"]:.3f}, respectively. In the CAP ≥248 dB/m subset (n={r["sensitivity_cap_subset"]["n"]:,}; {r["sensitivity_cap_subset"]["events12"]} events), AUROC was {r["sensitivity_cap_subset"]["auroc"]["FIB-4"]:.3f}, {r["sensitivity_cap_subset"]["auroc"]["Logistic"]:.3f} and {r["sensitivity_cap_subset"]["auroc"]["Gradient boosting"]:.3f}, respectively. CAP-based subgroup evaluation is descriptive and does not establish an etiologically verified MASLD population.')
 p(d,f'Removing GGT and HbA1c reduced the AUROC of the gradient-boosting approach from {gb["auroc"]:.3f} to {reduced["Gradient boosting"]["auroc"]:.3f}, while the reduced logistic model achieved {reduced["Logistic"]["auroc"]:.3f}. At 20% capacity, reduced-model case capture was {per(reduced["Gradient boosting"]["case_capture_at_20"])} for boosting and {per(reduced["Logistic"]["case_capture_at_20"])} for logistic regression. Conventional age-aware FIB-4 thresholds would have referred {per(fibrule["fraction_referred"])} of the weighted sample and captured {per(fibrule["case_capture"])} of cases; because the test budget differs, this observation is contextual rather than an estimate of matched advantage.')
