 add_section(d,'4. Discussion')
 p(d,f'The principal finding of this study was a material difference in retrospective case capture when prediction scores were translated into the same hypothetical diagnostic budget. In {v["n"]:,} temporally independent NHANES participants meeting the analytic eligibility criteria, calibrated gradient boosting captured approximately seven in ten people with LSM ≥12 kPa when 20% could receive VCTE, compared with approximately four in ten under a FIB-4 ranking. Importantly, this contrast was not obtained by granting additional testing capacity to the ML strategy; the estimated gain of {delta*100:.1f} percentage points resulted from different ordering of eligible individuals. The paired sampling interval did not cross zero, but neither the confidence interval nor the retrospective design measures prospective clinical benefit.')
 p(d,'These findings extend a distinct decision question from previously published NHANES-based ML investigations. Yue and colleagues studied explainable ML and a CAP-defined MASLD sample using these temporal survey periods, principally targeting significant fibrosis-related LSM ≥8 kPa [3]. Our cohort and endpoint are not identical; direct numerical comparisons with that study would therefore be misleading. Our narrower methodological contribution is to evaluate survey-weighted capacity-matched ranking, explicitly quantify the number of elevated-LSM cases potentially missed at a constrained quota, and audit allocation by demographic groups. A model with greater AUROC does not automatically imply proportional benefits at every operating point, and policy performance should be reported alongside discrimination and calibration [10–12].')
 p(d,'A noteworthy practical observation is the persistence of a large signal after removal of GGT and HbA1c. Although the nine-feature boosting model performed best at the main 20% quota, the reduced logistic model retained high discrimination and comparable capture. This supports further comparison of parsimonious predictors against the incremental laboratory expense and missing-data burden of extended models. Nevertheless, we did not measure local assay cost, availability, turnaround time, fairness of laboratory access or downstream costs. GGT and HbA1c cannot be assumed uniformly available in every setting, and clinical implementation would require a real resource inventory and prospective workflow study.')
 p(d,'Our results further illustrate why equity cannot be inferred from model-level AUROC. The age dependence of FIB-4 created large differences in hypothetical referrals between younger and older adults; boosted predictions moderated, but did not eliminate, this gradient. Even a higher-yield ML policy distributed referrals unequally by sex, while a simple equal-sex-rate benchmark produced little change in aggregate capture within an uncertainty interval consistent with either modest direction. A superficially fair rate of testing need not translate into equal opportunity to benefit when clinical need and underlying reference-outcome prevalence differ. In addition, some self-reported race/ethnicity categories had small outcome counts, and the estimates cannot be used to make robust racial fairness claims. Participatory specification of benefit, harm and access metrics should precede use of group-constrained clinical algorithms.')
 p(d,'The reported calibration pattern deserves attention. Although discrimination was comparatively strong, the weighted validation recalibration intercept for boosting was positive, suggesting systematically understated absolute event probabilities in the later sample. The prevalence of elevated LSM and composition of the sampled population can change with survey cycles, phlebotomy nonresponse, and clinical practice. Rank-based quota selection is invariant to monotone recalibration, but absolute risk counselling and threshold-based decisions are not. Separate external studies should test whether model recalibration, site-specific thresholds and practical measurement variability alter decisions before deployment [12].')
 p(d,'Several methodological constraints preclude diagnostic or causal claims. LSM is not histological staging, and elastography can be affected by hepatic inflammation, congestion, body habitus, probe issues and other liver diseases. We required technically complete NHANES examinations, but this also selectively excludes people with difficult or unavailable measurements. Moreover, the 12-kPa endpoint reflects high stiffness and possible advanced fibrosis, not adjudicated F3/F4, and metabolic risk alone does not establish MASLD. In particular, participants may have alcohol-related or viral liver disease; those etiologies were not exhaustively excluded with the available 14 files. Retrospective use of VCTE as the reference for everyone creates a convenient counterfactual comparison, yet a real clinic would not obtain these same outcomes in people who were never referred.')
 p(d,'The comparison is also not resource-equivalent at the predictor-acquisition stage: FIB-4 requires only age, AST, ALT and platelets, whereas the extended ML model uses additional information. We did not pre-select a clinical treatment threshold, account for age-dependent diagnostic pathways beyond the separate FIB-4 threshold sensitivity, or measure patient adherence, treatment outcomes and long-term cost effectiveness. Missing feature values were rare, but the analysis was restricted to laboratory-complete FIB-4 records and those with usable reference measurements; generalization to harder-to-reach or medically complex patients is uncertain. Predictor models were fitted without NHANES sampling weights, while evaluation used phlebotomy/examination weights; this represents one defensible predictive modelling strategy but is not a fully design-based estimation procedure.')
 p(d,'Finally, uncertainty intervals came from approximate masked-PSU resampling with fixed predictions; full uncertainty from model fitting, calibration, survey design and repeated hyperparameter searches was not included. The derivation and validation cohorts are temporally distinct samples from one US surveillance programme, not independent international clinical health systems. Separate, ethnically diverse, prospectively recruited clinical cohorts with adjudicated MASLD and fibrosis outcomes, explicit diagnostic-resource constraints and outcome follow-up are required. The current study should therefore be interpreted as a reproducible empirical feasibility and policy-evaluation analysis rather than a ready-to-use triage protocol.')
 add_section(d,'5. Conclusions')
 p(d,f'In publicly available NHANES cohorts of adults at metabolic risk, machine-learning rankings identified more individuals with elevated liver stiffness than FIB-4 when survey-weighted elastography capacity was hypothetically restricted. At a 20% capacity, gradient boosting captured {per(bg)} versus {per(fb)} of LSM ≥12 kPa cases, corresponding to a paired difference of {delta*100:.1f} percentage points. Performance gains persisted with a reduced predictor set, but gaps in referral opportunity remained, and simple sex-equalized allocation did not establish superior clinical equity. Linking prediction to transparent test-capacity and subgroup metrics offers a useful evaluation framework, but prospective validation with etiologically confirmed clinical outcomes is essential before any deployment.')
 add_section(d,'Supplementary Materials and Reproducibility')
 p(d,'The supplementary reproducibility package contains the original-source file manifest, executable Python scripts for model development and temporal validation, a secondary fairness/sensitivity audit, a generator for each figure, machine-readable aggregate results, and software requirements. The original CDC XPT files are not redistributed in the package but can be obtained from the URLs in the manifest. Random seeds, covariates, and model parameters are stated in the scripts. Participant-level prediction exports are intentionally excluded from the distributable bundle; they may be regenerated locally from original public NHANES data.')
